####################################################################################################
# PowerFramework -> .NET 10 decomposition -- ORCHESTRATION ENVIRONMENT TEMPLATE
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS -- AND WHAT DOES NOT EXIST YET
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The committed template for the *uncommitted* environment file that `orchestration/docker-compose.yml`
# WILL read. Its entire job is to declare the variable roster and explain each entry.
#
# THIS FILE IS PRESENT; THE MANIFEST THAT CONSUMES IT IS NOT. `orchestration/docker-compose.yml` has
# not been authored, and neither has `orchestration/README.md`. So no stack can be brought up today, and
# every `docker compose` command in this file -- including the one in the next block -- is the INTENDED
# bring-up that work will be built against rather than a step a reader can run now.
#
# ALL FOUR SERVICE IMAGES ARE BUILDABLE TODAY, WHICH IS A NARROWER CLAIM THAN IT SOUNDS.
# `services/gateway-service/Dockerfile`, `services/dataservices-service/Dockerfile`,
# `services/persistence-service/Dockerfile` and `services/security-service/Dockerfile` all exist, and
# each builds its own image from the repository root as its build context. So a reader can build and run
# four containers by hand today; what is still missing for the documented bring-up is the manifest that
# wires them together with the `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` readiness chain and the volume
# declaration the paired characterization captures depend on -- and neither can be substituted by
# hand-running containers, which is why the commands below remain intended rather than runnable.
#
# What IS present and checkable today is this roster: each variable below names the exact ASP.NET Core
# configuration key it maps onto, and every one of those keys was reconciled against the settings file
# that declares it.
#
# THIS FILE CARRIES NO REAL SECRET, AND IT NEVER MAY.
# Every value below is one of exactly six shapes: empty, a plain in-network address, a filesystem
# directory path, a boolean, an integer, or a bare identifier. There is no key, no certificate, no
# password and no token here -- not even a realistic-looking example of one. An example key is
# indistinguishable from a real one to a reader,
# and placeholder keys have a long history of reaching production unchanged, so the single secret in
# the roster is left EMPTY rather than pre-filled (docs/SECRETS.md section 4.1).
#
# EXACTLY ONE SIGNING SECRET EXISTS IN THE WHOLE SYSTEM: `SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY`.
# Security is the sole token issuer. Gateway, DataServices and Persistence hold VERIFICATION material
# only -- they validate inbound tokens with the framework's stock bearer handler against Security's
# published key set, and none of them can mint a token. You will not find a per-service signing key
# below under any spelling, and that is deliberate: a service able to mint its own tokens would be a
# second issuer, and the security properties of a sole-issuer topology depend on there being one.
#
# HOW IT WILL BE USED -- the intended flow, once the manifest exists
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The documented default keeps the populated file OUTSIDE the working tree -- see the warning in the
# next block for why that matters more than it looks like it should:
#
#   install -d -m 700 "$HOME/.config/powerframework"
#   cp orchestration/.env.example "$HOME/.config/powerframework/pfw.env"
#   chmod 600 "$HOME/.config/powerframework/pfw.env"
#   # populate SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY in that file -- it is an RSA PRIVATE KEY, not random bytes.
#   # The generation command, and why random bytes are rejected at startup, are under that variable
#   # in section 1. Do not reach for `openssl rand`: Security signs with RS256 and nothing else.
#   cd orchestration && docker compose --env-file "$HOME/.config/powerframework/pfw.env" up --build -d
#
# FOUR THINGS HAVE TO BE PREPARED FOR A LOCAL BRING-UP, all of them in section 1 and all of them
# generated rather than chosen: the signing key, and one issuance-roster secret per caller that may
# obtain a token (three of them). Every other default below is already the working local value.
#
# Generate them with, in order:
#
#     openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER | base64 -w0
#     openssl rand -base64 32      # once per roster secret, a DIFFERENT value each time
#
# A MISSING ONE REFUSES THE HOST RATHER THAN DEGRADING IT, which is the intended way to find out: the
# refusal names the configuration key, or the roster position, and never a value.
#
# A CERTIFICATE SET *IS* REQUIRED, BECAUSE ALL FOUR LISTENERS TERMINATE TLS. Every boundary in this
# stack is created by the decomposition itself and every request across one carries a bearer token,
# while Security additionally publishes the key set the whole estate verifies against -- so cleartext
# would make every token replayable and the trust bootstrap substitutable on path (CWE-319). The
# attached environment gates readiness on `curl -sf http://localhost:<port>/health` and supplies no
# certificate material; neither fact licenses cleartext. The gate is a PROBE SHAPE, restatable as
# `curl -sf --cacert <anchor> https://localhost:<port>/health`, and the absent material is supplied the
# way every other secret is: from the deployment's own secret layer through TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and
# TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH in section 5, with nothing committed here (C-F).
#
# The MUTUAL-TLS variables below remain in the roster but are OPTIONAL and EMPTY by
# default: they are the CALLER-identity half, for a deployment that wants a client certificate on the
# issuance edge, and setting them requires no code change anywhere. The SERVER halves -- which every
# listener needs -- are TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH in section 5. The generation
# commands for both are in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 9.3.1.
#
# WHAT AUTHENTICATES TOKEN ISSUANCE, GIVEN THAT THE TRANSPORT AUTHENTICATES NOBODY. TLS establishes that
# the channel is private, never who is on the other end of it, so `POST /v1/tokens` requires a caller
# credential on top of it: either an HTTP `Basic` credential naming a subject on Security's issuance
# roster, which works on every topology because the operation reads the header itself, or a client
# certificate wherever Security terminates the handshake directly. A request presenting NEITHER is
# refused with `401`, so the boundary is authenticated in both senses (C-G).
#
# FILE FORMAT -- read by Docker Compose's dotenv parser, NOT by a shell
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plain `KEY=value` lines and whole-line `#` comments only. No `export`, no quoting, no command
# substitution, no `$` interpolation, no line continuation, no inline trailing comments. Values are
# taken literally, including any spaces, so nothing here is quoted and nothing needs to be. Reserved
# URI characters are safe unquoted -- `:`, `/`, `?`, `&`, `+` and `=` all pass through verbatim, which is
# why the service addresses need no quoting and why the long single-line base64 signing key in section 1
# does not either. The corollary matters as much: with no quoting there is no LINE CONTINUATION, so every
# value must fit on one line and a multi-line PEM block cannot be expressed in this file at all.
#
# IF A KEY APPEARS TWICE, THE LATER LINE WINS. That is why every commented deployed-form alternate below
# sits immediately AFTER the active line it replaces: uncommenting one takes effect even if you forget to
# comment out the line above it. Comment the line above out anyway, so the file states one value per key
# and the next reader is not left deducing precedence.
#
# THE PARITY RULE BETWEEN THIS FILE AND THE COMPOSE MANIFEST
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The roster here and the `${...}` references in `orchestration/docker-compose.yml` must match EXACTLY,
# in both directions. A variable the manifest references but this file does not declare makes bring-up
# fail, or worse, silently resolve to an empty value; a variable declared here that the manifest never
# references is dead configuration that misleads whoever edits it. Every entry below therefore names the
# ASP.NET Core configuration key it is mapped onto -- `__` is the environment-variable spelling of the
# `:` section separator -- so the mapping is a stated contract rather than something to be inferred.
#
# THE MANIFEST DOES NOT EXIST YET, SO THIS RULE IS CURRENTLY AN OBLIGATION ON IT RATHER THAN A CHECKED
# INVARIANT. It is stated here, before the roster, because this file is the side that already exists:
# whoever authors the manifest reconciles it against this roster, and the reconciliation is checkable in
# one direction only until then.
#
# WHERE TO READ MORE
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   orchestration/README.md   bring-up, the ordered readiness gates, and teardown (PLANNED - absent)
#   docs/SECRETS.md           the secrets register: locators, severities, token topology, follow-ups
#   docs/BUILD.md             section 8, local orchestration, and the per-service build commands
#   docs/ARCHITECTURE.md      the port map, transport per service, and the capability gate
####################################################################################################


####################################################################################################
# WARNING -- THE IGNORE RULES DO NOT COVER AN ENVIRONMENT FILE. READ BEFORE GENERATING A KEY.
#
# THIS IS A VERIFIED GAP, NOT A HYPOTHETICAL ONE. The repository-root `.gitignore` contains exactly
# these entries and no others: /pack/*, /packuse/*, /blink/*, /bak/*, /*.usr.opt, /**/pfw.dev.*,
# *.dmp, *.log, *.bak, *.bat, *.zip, *.rar, thumbs.db, !/res/*.zip, !/samples/*.zip.
#
# There is NO `.env` pattern among them, and this refactor does not add one -- the root ignore file is
# left exactly as the legacy repository has it. One nested ignore file does exist, `tests/e2e/`
# .gitignore, but it applies only beneath that directory and has no effect on `orchestration/`.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: a populated `orchestration/.env` holding the system's single
# signing key would be an ordinary untracked file inside the working tree. `git add -A` would stage
# it and a careless commit would publish it. Being untracked is NOT a control; it is one command away
# from being tracked, and history is forever -- material committed to version control must be
# considered exposed for as long as that history is retained.
#
# SO PICK ONE OF THESE TWO, AND DO IT BEFORE YOU WRITE A KEY ANYWHERE:
#
#   1. PREFERRED -- keep the populated file outside the working tree entirely, on a path version
#      control cannot reach, and point Compose at it with `--env-file`. This is the flow in the header
#      block above and the one docs/BUILD.md section 8 documents as the default.
#
#   2. IN-TREE -- the attached environment's own `cp .env.example .env` form remains supported, but
#      only with a local exclusion added FIRST:
#
#        set -euo pipefail
#        echo 'orchestration/.env' >> .git/info/exclude
#        cd orchestration
#        [ -f .env ] || cp .env.example .env
#
#      `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone and untracked, so it excludes the file without editing the
#      read-only root ignore file. Guard the copy exactly as shown: an unguarded `cp` silently
#      overwrites a populated `.env` with this template, destroying the key and leaving a stack that
#      starts and then rejects every token -- a failure whose cause is nowhere near its symptom. Keep
#      `cd` on its OWN line under `set -euo pipefail` rather than chaining it with `&&` before an `||`:
#      in a `cd x && test || cp` list a failing `cd` does NOT abort the shell, and the copy then lands
#      in whatever directory you were actually standing in. On its own line, a failed `cd` stops the
#      script before anything is written.
#
# ONE CONTROL THAT IS ALREADY IN PLACE, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER: the repository-root
# `.dockerignore` excludes `orchestration/` outright plus `**/.env` and `**/.env.*`, so no environment
# file can enter a build context or an image layer -- this template included. That control is about
# IMAGES. It does nothing about version control, and it is not a substitute for either option above.
# Nor are filesystem permissions secret management: the deployment path for real key material is the
# orchestration secret layer, injected as environment configuration and never written into the
# repository at all.
####################################################################################################


####################################################################################################
# 1. SECURITY -- `security-service`, ONE listener, `https://+:5104`, `Http1`. THE SOLE TOKEN ISSUER.
#    TLS is functional here rather than hardening: the issuance response body IS a credential and the
#    published key set is the whole estate's trust bootstrap, so a readable channel makes the first
#    replayable and the second substitutable. The documented readiness gate adapts -- it becomes
#    `curl -sf --cacert <anchor> https://localhost:5104/health` -- because a probe command is a
#    documentation detail while a substitutable trust bootstrap is not restatable at all. `Http1` rather
#    than `Http1AndHttp2` because Security publishes no gRPC contract, and a listener that accepts only
#    what it is for cannot be misaddressed silently.
#
#    ISSUANCE IS STILL AUTHENTICATED, BY A CREDENTIAL RATHER THAN BY THE TRANSPORT. `POST /v1/tokens`
#    accepts either an HTTP `Basic` credential naming a subject on Security's issuance roster -- which
#    works on every topology, because the operation reads the header itself -- or a client certificate
#    wherever Security terminates the handshake directly. Presenting neither is `401`. One listener therefore carries the
#    credential-authenticated issuance operation, the bearer-authenticated C-02 surface and the
#    anonymous `/health` at once, which is what the per-operation security requirements in
#    shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/OpenApi/security.v1.yaml express. See
#    SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH below for the certificate half.
#
# Security mints short-lived service tokens and publishes the verification material the other three
# services trust. That publication is why Security speaks REST rather than gRPC: a stock bearer handler
# consumes `GET /.well-known/jwks.json` and `GET /.well-known/openid-configuration` and self-configures
# with ZERO bespoke code, which keeps signature checking, key rollover, issuer and audience validation
# and clock-skew handling inside framework code instead of hand-written code. Choosing gRPC here would
# have forced custom key-set retrieval into three separate services -- a net increase in hand-written
# security-critical code, which is the wrong direction.
#
# Both publication endpoints are anonymous, because verification material is public by design. That is
# exactly what distinguishes it from the signing key below.
####################################################################################################

# SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY -- THE ONLY SIGNING SECRET IN THE ENTIRE SYSTEM.
#
# Consumed by:  Security only  ->  the FLAT configuration key `SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY`
# Mandatory:    YES. The Compose manifest references it in the required form, so bring-up ABORTS with
#               a named error rather than starting a stack that would reject every token.
# Default:      NONE. Deliberately left EMPTY -- see the header block for why no example value appears.
#
# THIS ONE VARIABLE DOES NOT FOLLOW THE `__` SECTION-SEPARATOR CONVENTION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
# Every other entry in this file maps onto a section path -- `__` being the environment spelling of the
# `:` separator. This one binds as a FLAT key of exactly the literal name above, because `SECURITY_JWT_`
# would otherwise be read as three nested sections and the value would never land on the options
# instance. Security therefore reads it explicitly by name rather than by conventional section binding,
# and the name is fixed: do NOT rewrite it as `Security__SigningKey` or add such an alias.
#
# IT IS AN RSA PRIVATE KEY, NOT RANDOM BYTES. Security signs with RS256 -- RSA + SHA-256 + PKCS#1 v1.5
# -- and the algorithm is a CLOSED allow-list of RS256, RS384 and RS512. Random bytes cannot be imported
# as an asymmetric private key, so material produced by `openssl rand` fails the startup usability check
# and THE HOST REFUSES TO START, with a message that names this variable and never echoes the value.
# Measured, so this is not a caution but a fact: base64 of 32 random bytes and base64 of 64 random bytes
# are both REJECTED by the import path, while both forms generated below are accepted at 2048 bits.
#
# GENERATE ONE LOCALLY, AT DEPLOYMENT TIME. It is not provided by any platform:
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER | base64 -w0
#
# Paste that output as the value on ONE line. This file's format has no line continuation and no quoting
# (see FILE FORMAT in the header), so a multi-line PEM block CANNOT be expressed here -- the single-line
# base64-of-DER form is the shape this variable takes. Expect roughly 1.6 KB of base64 for a 2048-bit
# key; that is normal for an environment variable and needs no wrapping. `-w0` is GNU coreutils; the
# portable spelling is `... | openssl base64 -A`.
#
# THE PEM FORM IS ALSO ACCEPTED, for the deployment path where the value arrives from a secret store
# that can carry newlines rather than from this template:
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out pfw-signing.pem   # chmod 600 it
#
# Security tries a PEM import first and falls back to base64-DER, so both PEM encodings are usable: the
# PKCS#8 form the command above emits, and the older PKCS#1 form an existing key may already be in. The
# PEM delimiter lines are deliberately not reproduced anywhere in this file, per the header's rule that
# not even a realistic-looking example of key material appears here -- and a delimiter is what every
# secret scanner matches on, so writing one would trip the scan that protects this file. That dual
# acceptance mirrors the legacy generator, whose PEM output is an OPTIONAL fourth argument
# [ws_objects/pfw.crypto.pbl.src/n_crypto.sru:L19-L20], so legacy private-key material exists in both
# shapes and neither may be refused.
#
# THE PUBLIC HALF IS NEVER CONFIGURED. Security derives it from this key and publishes it at
# `/.well-known/jwks.json`; there is no public-key variable to set and no key to distribute by hand. If
# you need the public half out of band, `openssl pkey -in pfw-signing.pem -pubout` prints it.
#
# USE 2048 BITS OR MORE, AND NOTHING IN THIS DEPLOYMENT ENFORCES THAT FOR YOU -- SO IT IS THE ONE LINE IN
# THIS BLOCK THAT IS YOUR RESPONSIBILITY RATHER THAN THE HOST'S. An earlier form of this file said the
# floor was enforced by a `Security:SigningKeyMinimumSizeBits` setting. There is no such leaf and no
# key-size check anywhere on the signing path: `Tokens/SigningKeyProvider.cs` imports the key, confirms
# it is a usable RSA private key, and asks nothing about its modulus. A 1024-bit key handed to this
# variable would be ACCEPTED and would become the trust root all three verifiers validate every token
# against. Saying otherwise was worse than saying nothing, because it moved the responsibility to a
# mechanism that does not exist.
#
# The generation command above produces 2048 by default, which is why following it needs no further
# thought. 2048 is also a first-class legacy size [ws_objects/pfw.shared.pbl.src/enums.sru:L966], as is
# 4096 at [:L967], so a larger key is equally at home here.
#
# WHY A SMALLER KEY IS STILL A BAD IDEA HERE EVEN THOUGH THE LEGACY ALLOWS ONE, WHICH IS WORTH STATING
# BECAUSE THE ALLOWANCE LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD APPLY. The legacy enum does keep `CRYPTO_RSA_BITS_1024 = 1024` as a first-class value at
# [:L965], and that weakness IS preserved -- on the C-02 crypto surface, where the CALLER supplies the
# size and byte-for-byte parity with `n_crypto`'s `GenRSAKey` is the obligation. This variable is a
# different kind of thing: it is the system's OWN signing identity, the trust root all three verifiers
# validate every token against, and it is NET-NEW -- the legacy framework has no token issuer at all, so
# there is no legacy behaviour here to preserve or to correct. Applying the C-02 allowance to the trust
# root would not be parity; it would be importing a weakness from a surface that has nothing to do with
# it. If you need a 1024-bit key, the C-02 key-generation operation still produces one -- and that is the
# surface to ask for it on, precisely because this one will not stop you.
#
# FORMAT IS ENFORCED TOO, FOR THE SAME REASON. `Security:SigningKeyFormat` is `PemOrPkcs8Base64`, and the
# name states the ACCEPTANCE ORDER as well as the closed set: PEM is tried first -- both the PKCS#8 form
# and the older PKCS#1 form -- then base64 of the DER encoding of a PKCS#8 private key on ONE line, which
# is precisely what the `openssl genpkey ... -outform DER | base64 -w0` command above produces and the
# only shape THIS file can hold, since the dotenv format has no line continuation. PEM stays accepted for
# the deployment path where the value arrives from a secret store that can carry newlines, and neither
# shape may be refused: legacy private-key material genuinely exists in both, the generator's PEM output
# being an optional fourth argument [ws_objects/pfw.crypto.pbl.src/n_crypto.sru:L19-L20]. An earlier
# revision of this note named the value `Pkcs8Base64` and described one accepted shape; that was the
# wrong spelling and the wrong count, and a value outside the closed set is now refused at startup. A
# value in an environment file gets one chance to be right, so the shape is checked at startup rather
# than described here and hoped for.
#
# DO NOT "FIX" A REJECTED RANDOM STRING BY SWITCHING THE ALGORITHM TO AN HMAC FAMILY. The setting
# `Security:SigningAlgorithm` accepts no HS* value, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic:
# the JWK set Security publishes is anonymous verification material, so an HMAC key there would be
# publishing the signing secret itself, and handing it to the three verifiers would make each of them a
# co-signer. The sole-issuer property this whole topology rests on would be gone. Generate the RSA key.
#
# ROTATION PAIRS WITH THE KEY ID. `Security:SigningKeyId` is the `kid` stamped into every token header
# and published in the JWK set, so a new key gets a new id and the two are changed together; that is what
# lets the old and new keys coexist in the set while tokens minted under the old one are still in flight.
#
# Held by Security and by nothing else. It appears in no source file, in no `appsettings.json`, in no
# `appsettings.Development.json` and in no container definition; the only path by which it reaches the
# process is configuration injection from this orchestration secret layer.
SECURITY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ISSUANCE-ROSTER SECRETS -- ONE PER CALLER THAT MAY OBTAIN A TOKEN. REQUIRED (C-G).
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THESE EXIST. `POST /v1/tokens` is the one operation a bearer token cannot protect, because a caller
# cannot present a token in order to obtain its first one. A client certificate is the alternative and it
# is not always presentable -- anything that re-terminates TLS in front of Security strips it -- so the
# primary credential is a shared secret, sent as an HTTP `Basic` credential whose user-id is the caller's
# service identity and
# whose password is the value of the variable below. Security resolves that identity against its issuance
# roster (`Security:Clients` in its settings file), which ALSO decides which audiences and which scopes
# that identity may ask for. Authentication and authorisation of a caller are one configured decision
# about one caller, which is why they are one section there and one variable each here.
#
# EACH VARIABLE IS NAMED BY THE ROSTER AND CARRIES ONLY THE VALUE. The settings file declares the NAME
# (`SecretConfigurationKey`), never the material -- the same indirection `Security:KeyStore` uses -- so no
# credential is committed anywhere (C-F).
#
# GENERATE ONE PER CALLER, AND DO NOT SHARE ONE ACROSS TWO. Sharing collapses two identities into one
# credential, and the permission model then grants each caller the other's audiences and scopes in
# practice even though the roster says otherwise:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# ALL THREE ARE REQUIRED FOR THE DOCUMENTED BRING-UP, and a missing one REFUSES THE HOST rather than
# degrading it -- and now on BOTH SIDES of the exchange. Security resolves every secret its roster names
# at startup and reports the roster POSITION of any that resolves to nothing. Independently, each CALLING
# service refuses to start when it can present neither accepted scheme: Gateway checks
# SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_GATEWAY against its own client-certificate pair, and DataServices checks
# SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_DATASERVICES against its own, each naming both keys and quoting neither value.
# Two independent refusals rather than one, because the two failures are different -- an unregistered
# secret is the issuer's configuration and an absent one is the caller's. That is deliberate: the alternative is a caller that
# mysteriously cannot authenticate against a service whose readiness probe reports healthy - the failure
# shape this whole layer's fail-fast validation exists to avoid, and the posture the legacy application
# object sets by ending a structural fault in termination rather than a warning
# [ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L111-L144].
#
# WHY THREE AND NOT FIVE. Gateway obtains tokens for DataServices; DataServices obtains them for
# Persistence and for Security's cryptographic surface. PERSISTENCE HAS NO ENTRY AND NEEDS NO SECRET -- it
# requests no token at all, and reads Security's published key set anonymously. The third is the
# operator/end-to-end identity, registered only in `appsettings.Development.json`, which is the
# environment `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT` below selects: `tests/e2e` presents it as
# `SECURITY_CLIENT_ID` / `SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET`, and SECURITY_CLIENT_ID MUST BE SET TO
# `pfw-e2e-suite` because the issuance edge reconciles the claimed subject against the authenticated
# credential identity ordinally -- a mismatch is a `403`, by design.
SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_GATEWAY=
SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET_DATASERVICES=
SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# SECURITY_MTLS_CERT_PATH / SECURITY_MTLS_KEY_PATH -- SECURITY'S SERVER CERTIFICATE, FOR A DEPLOYMENT
# THAT CHOOSES TO TERMINATE TLS HERE. Paths, never material.
#
# Consumed by:  nothing, and DELIBERATELY SO -- this entry exists to say where the server half went.
# Mandatory:    NO -- not because there is no handshake, but because Security's server half is supplied
#               through the SAME variables every other listener uses rather than through a
#               service-specific pair: TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH in section 5,
#               which bind to Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path and __KeyPath. What a deployment adds
#               HERE is `ClientCertificateMode` `AllowCertificate` on the endpoint, which is what makes
#               the mutual-TLS caller alternative reachable. An earlier revision of this
#               template declared a second, Security-specific pair against
#               `Kestrel__Endpoints__Mtls__Certificate__*` for a separate mutual-TLS listener on 5154.
#               That listener never existed: Security declares ONE endpoint, so the certificate would be
#               the default one and the client-certificate negotiation a property of that endpoint
#               rather than of a second.
#
# HOW THE TOKEN ENDPOINT AUTHENTICATES WITHOUT A LISTENER OF ITS OWN, AND WITHOUT TLS. Contract C-01
# requires a caller credential on `POST /v1/tokens` because a caller cannot present a bearer token in
# order to obtain its first bearer token, and it publishes TWO schemes for it, either of which
# satisfies the operation:
#
#   * `clientCredential` -- an HTTP `Basic` credential naming a subject on Security's issuance roster.
#     This is the primary scheme, because the operation reads the header itself and so needs no
#     cooperation from whatever terminated the handshake.
#   * `mutualTls` -- a client certificate. Reachable only where TLS is terminated at this listener,
#     which is the fallback AAP section 0.6.6.3 describes for that one pair. Where it is configured,
#     `AllowCertificate` makes Kestrel REQUEST and validate a certificate without DEMANDING one, so the
#     certificate reaches the token operation's authorization policy while `/health` stays anonymously
#     probeable and the C-02 operations stay bearer-authenticated on the same listener.
#     `RequireCertificate` at the LISTENER would instead break three contracts to enforce one.
#
# A request presenting NEITHER is refused with `401` by the operation, which is what
# `OpenApi/security.v1.yaml` publishes. Authentication is therefore a property of the operation on
# every topology, rather than a property of the transport on one of them.

# SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH -- THE AUTHORITY WHOSE CLIENT CERTIFICATES SECURITY TRUSTS. A path.
#
# Consumed by:  Security  ->  Security__MutualTls__ClientCaPath
#
#               An EARLIER revision of this file said this variable was consumed by the Compose
#               manifest and the container definition rather than by a configuration key, on the
#               reasoning that WHICH issuers are acceptable is the container's OS trust store. That was
#               wrong in a way that made the whole mutual-TLS edge inoperable, and the correction is
#               worth stating rather than quietly applying:
#
#                 * The anchor never reached any trust store. No container definition installed it and
#                   no `update-ca-certificates` step existed, so the store the reasoning relied on was
#                   empty of it -- which meant every client certificate issued by the documented local
#                   authority was rejected during the handshake.
#                 * Installing it into the OS store would need a root-privileged step in a runtime
#                   image that deliberately runs as a non-root user.
#                 * And `POST /v1/tokens` derives the caller's identity from the presented
#                   certificate's COMMON NAME. A name proves nothing unless the chain behind it is
#                   verified, so with no anchor anywhere a caller could have presented a self-signed
#                   certificate named `powerframework-gateway` and been minted a Gateway token.
#
#               So the anchor is now a real configuration key. Security loads it at startup and
#               installs Kestrel's `ClientCertificateValidation` callback, which builds the caller's
#               chain under `X509ChainTrustMode.CustomRootTrust` against this anchor and nothing else.
#               `AllowCertificate` still only REQUESTS a certificate, so `/health`, the published key
#               set and the discovery document stay anonymously reachable on the same listener.
# Mandatory:    YES wherever the issuance endpoint must accept a caller. Unset means the platform's own
#               trust store decides, which is correct only where the caller CA is already installed in
#               the image. A path that is set but unreadable is a structural fault and Security refuses
#               to start rather than accept callers on an unverified name.
# Default:      the mount point below. Mount the PUBLIC half of the CA -- never its key: an anchor with
#               its private key beside it would let Security issue the very identities it authenticates.
SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH=

# SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_REVOCATION_MODE -- HOW THOROUGHLY A PRESENTED CLIENT CERTIFICATE'S REVOCATION IS
# CHECKED. One of `NoCheck`, `Offline` or `Online`. Not a secret and not a path.
#
# Consumed by:  Security  ->  Security__ClientCertificateRevocationMode
#
# SEPARATE FROM THE ANCHOR ABOVE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. The two answer different questions and a
# deployment can honestly answer one without the other: the anchor says WHOSE certificates are
# acceptable, this says whether the issuer's revocation data has to be REACHABLE before one is accepted.
# A deployment can trust an issuer it cannot reach - an air-gapped network, a CRL distribution point
# behind an egress policy - and folding the two into one setting would force such a deployment to choose
# between refusing every caller and disabling the anchor entirely.
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS `NoCheck`, AND IT IS THE SETTINGS FILE'S OWN DEFAULT RATHER THAN A SECOND OPINION.
# `services/security-service/PowerFramework.Security/appsettings.json` declares `NoCheck` and records the
# reason: an air-gapped or offline deployment has no distribution point to reach, and a mode that could
# not be satisfied would turn every caller into a refusal. The two values here and there are deliberately
# the same, because a template default that disagreed with the shipped one would silently change
# behaviour for every deployment that copied this file without editing it.
#
# `Offline` and `Online` are the two stricter values and a deployment with reachable revocation data
# should name one. Prefer `Offline` over `Online` unless the responder's availability is itself part of
# the deployment's guarantees: online checking makes the issuance edge depend on a third party's CRL or
# OCSP responder being up, so a responder outage stops every service in the estate obtaining a token and
# surfaces as an unexplained 401 rather than as an outage of the thing that is actually down.
# Mandatory:    NO. Unset takes the service's own default, which is the same value.
# Default:      NoCheck
SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_REVOCATION_MODE=NoCheck

# INTERNAL_TLS_TRUSTED_CA_PATH -- THE AUTHORITY WHOSE SERVER CERTIFICATES THE OTHER THREE SERVICES
# TRUST. A path. The mirror image of the variable above: that one says whose CLIENT certificates
# Security accepts, this one says whose SERVER certificates a caller accepts.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway      ->  Gateway__InternalTls__TrustedCaPath
#               DataServices ->  DataServices__InternalTls__TrustedCaPath
#               Persistence  ->  InternalTls__TrustedCaPath
#
#               Persistence's key has no service prefix because its configuration root is the file
#               itself rather than a named section, which is the same shape its `Jwt` and `Sqlite`
#               sections already have.
#
# WHY IT IS FUNCTIONAL RATHER THAN HARDENING. All four listeners in this stack terminate TLS -- 5101,
# 5102, 5104 and 5105 -- with a certificate issued by the LOCAL authority docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 9.3.1
# generates, and
# that authority is in no container's OS trust store. Left on platform default trust every internal
# channel refuses the certificate it is presented, and the list of what stops working is the whole
# system: Gateway cannot obtain a token or reach DataServices, DataServices cannot reach any of the four
# Persistence RPCs, and all three bearer handlers fail to fetch the published key set -- so every inbound
# token is refused for want of a key rather than on its merits, which reads to an operator as a token
# problem when it is a trust problem.
#
# IT NARROWS TRUST; IT DOES NOT RELAX IT. Under `X509ChainTrustMode.CustomRootTrust` the mounted anchor
# becomes the ONLY acceptable root for internal traffic and the machine's several hundred public roots
# stop being acceptable for it. Chain building, name validation and validity dates stay with the
# platform. No service in this repository installs a validation callback that accepts an invalid
# certificate, and none calls `DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificate` (C-G).
#
# Mandatory:    YES for any topology whose internal certificates come from a local authority, which is
#               every topology this repository documents. Unset means platform default trust, which is
#               correct where internal certificates are publicly issued or the anchor is already in the
#               image. Set-but-unreadable is a structural fault: the service refuses to start.
# Default:      the mount point below. The PUBLIC half only.
#
# REVOCATION IS NOT CHECKED ON THESE CHANNELS, and that follows from the topology rather than from
# convenience. A CA created by two `openssl` invocations publishes no revocation list and runs no
# responder, so an online check has nothing to ask and would make every internal handshake wait for a
# lookup that must fail. The 30-day certificate lifetime in the generation recipe is the control that
# substitutes for it. A deployment whose authority does publish revocation information leaves this unset
# and uses platform trust, where the platform's own revocation behaviour applies.
INTERNAL_TLS_TRUSTED_CA_PATH=/run/secrets/internal-tls/ca.crt

# GATEWAY_MTLS_CERT_PATH / GATEWAY_MTLS_KEY_PATH -- GATEWAY'S CLIENT HALF OF THE ISSUANCE HANDSHAKE.
# DATASERVICES_MTLS_CERT_PATH / DATASERVICES_MTLS_KEY_PATH -- DATASERVICES' CLIENT HALF. All four are
# PATHS.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway      ->  Gateway__MutualTls__CertificatePath
#                                Gateway__MutualTls__CertificateKeyPath
#               DataServices ->  DataServices__Security__MutualTls__CertificatePath
#                                DataServices__Security__MutualTls__CertificateKeyPath
# Mandatory:    NO. These are the `mutualTls` half of the issuance edge, and they apply only where
#               Security terminates the handshake itself and requests a certificate. Wherever that does
#               not hold, a caller authenticates to `POST /v1/tokens` with its `Basic` roster credential
#               instead; the trust anchor above says whose certificates Security would accept, and
#               these say which certificate each caller would present.
# Default:      EMPTY -- ALL FOUR, AND THAT IS LOAD BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY. Both-empty is a
#               documented, supported state meaning "this deployment presents no client certificate".
#               A pre-filled mount point is NOT: `Program.cs` loads the pair eagerly with
#               `X509Certificate2.CreateFromPemFile` and turns an `IOException` into a startup failure,
#               so a default naming a file no manifest mounts would stop Gateway and DataServices from
#               starting on the very bring-up this template documents. The mount points a
#               TLS-terminating deployment would use are
#               /run/secrets/security-mtls/<service>-client.crt and .key. They are PATHS either way: no
#               certificate and no private key is committed to this repository or embedded in any image
#               (C-F).
#
# BOTH-OR-NEITHER, PER SERVICE, AND THAT IS ENFORCED RATHER THAN DOCUMENTED. Each options group refuses
# a half-configured pair at startup with a names-only message: a certificate cannot complete a handshake
# without its key, and a key has nothing to present without its certificate. Leaving a pair entirely
# unset is a legitimate state and is deliberately not a startup failure. Half-set is a typo and fails
# loudly.
#
# UNSET IS NOT A STARTUP FAILURE, BUT IT IS NOT READY EITHER, AND THE TWO ARE DIFFERENT STATEMENTS. A
# service that cannot reach the issuance edge cannot obtain a token, and every operation it forwards
# carries one - so it can serve nothing. Both callers therefore report `Degraded` on `/health` while
# their pair is unset, which is a 503, which holds the Compose dependency gate shut. That is the point:
# readiness exists to express the gap between "did not refuse to start" and "can serve", and a 200 there
# would open the gate onto an instance that refuses every request. Each service names the unmet setting
# in a `credentials` component entry of its own `/health` body, so an operator sees which pair is
# missing rather than only that something is.
#
# Generate the local set with the commands in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 9.3.1 -- a local CA, the
# server certificate of section 5, and one client certificate per calling service. Rotation is a file
# replacement and a restart; no code change and no rebuild.
GATEWAY_MTLS_CERT_PATH=
GATEWAY_MTLS_KEY_PATH=
DATASERVICES_MTLS_CERT_PATH=
DATASERVICES_MTLS_KEY_PATH=

# SECURITY_E2E_CALLER -- the END-TO-END SUITE'S ROW IN SECURITY'S AUTHORIZATION MATRIX.
#
# Consumed by:  Security  ->  Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Caller
#                             Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Audience
#                             Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Scopes__0 .. __2
# Mandatory:    NO for a deployment. YES for running `tests/e2e`, which cannot obtain a token without it.
#
# WHY THIS IS HERE RATHER THAN IN `appsettings.json`. That file ships the matrix's PRODUCTION rows --
# Gateway to DataServices, DataServices to Persistence, DataServices to Security -- read directly off the
# outbound clients that make those requests, so a clean bring-up is both fail-closed and working. The
# end-to-end suite's identity is NOT required for production operation, so shipping a grant for it in the
# settings file would authorise a harness identity in every deployment that reads that file. It is
# deployment configuration for the bring-up, and this is where the bring-up's configuration lives.
#
# THE INDEX 3 IS NOT ARBITRARY, AND GETTING IT WRONG FAILS SILENTLY. `appsettings.json` declares rows 0,
# 1 and 2; the configuration provider merges an array BY INDEX rather than by appending, so 3 is the first
# free index. This was MEASURED rather than assumed, and the measurement is worth recording because the
# failure mode is worse than a lost row: setting index 0 here produced a HYBRID row carrying this file's
# caller with `appsettings.json`'s audience and scopes -- a real production grant silently redirected to a
# different caller, with the row count unchanged and nothing refused. The service's duplicate-pair guard
# does NOT catch it, because a hybrid is not a duplicate.
#
# SO IF A FOURTH ROW IS EVER ADDED TO `appsettings.json`, THIS INDEX MUST MOVE WITH IT. Nothing at runtime
# will tell you: the new row would be wholly shadowed by the values below. The guard against forgetting is
# a test rather than a startup check -- `ShippedCallerAuthorizationMatrixTests` in the Security suite pins
# the settings file to exactly three rows in a fixed order and names this variable in its failure, so
# adding a row breaks that test before it can reach a deployment.
#
# IT GRANTS NOTHING WITHOUT A CERTIFICATE. Security reconciles the request's `subject` against the common
# name of the presented client certificate BEFORE it consults this matrix, so this row is reachable only
# by a caller holding a certificate for that name from the authority named at SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH
# -- which the deployment controls. Generate one alongside the other client certificates using the
# commands in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 9.3.1, with the common name set to the caller below.
#
# THE VALUES ARE READ FROM THE SUITE, NOT INVENTED HERE. `tests/e2e/fixtures/auth.ts` declares the caller
# as `E2E_TOKEN_SUBJECT`, the audience as `DEFAULT_TOKEN_AUDIENCE` (Gateway, the sole ingress and the only
# service the suite calls) and the three scopes as `DEFAULT_TOKEN_SCOPES`, one per Gateway surface the
# specs actually call. Keep the two in step: a scope the suite requests and this row omits is NOT an
# error - the request succeeds with a narrower granted set - so a mismatch surfaces as an authorization
# failure at Gateway rather than at issuance.
Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Caller=pfw-e2e-suite
Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Audience=powerframework-gateway
Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Scopes__0=ping
Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Scopes__1=capabilities
Security__CallerAuthorizations__3__Scopes__2=datawindow

# SECURITY_JWT_ISSUER -- the `iss` claim Security stamps, and the issuer the three verifiers require.
#
# Consumed by:  Security (minting)  ->  Security__Issuer
#               Gateway (validation)  ->  Authentication__Schemes__Bearer__ValidIssuers__0
#               DataServices and Persistence (validation) compare the `iss` claim against the issuer
#                   their bearer authority's discovery document publishes, so they need no issuer
#                   setting of their own -- they need the AUTHORITY to be Security. See the note on
#                   per-service section names under SECURITY_BASE_URL below.
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up
#
# An identifier, not a secret. It must be BYTE-IDENTICAL on the minting side and on all three
# validating sides, or every token is rejected with an issuer mismatch. Keep it equal to
# `SECURITY_BASE_URL` below -- INCLUDING THE SCHEME -- and change the two together. The scheme here is
# `http` because that is the scheme Security's listener binds (see SECURITY_BASE_URL); an issuer spelled
# `https` while the service publishes `http` is an issuer mismatch that rejects every token, and the
# mismatch is silent until the first authenticated request.
SECURITY_JWT_ISSUER=https://security-service:5104

# SECURITY_JWT_AUDIENCE -- the `aud` claim for tokens issued to reach the ingress.
#
# Consumed by:  Security (minting) and Gateway (validation)
#               ->  Authentication__Schemes__Bearer__ValidAudiences__0 on Gateway
# Mandatory:    no -- this default matches the value Gateway's own `appsettings.json` already carries
#
# An identifier, not a secret. Only the INGRESS audience is templated here, because it is the one an
# operator may have to align with an external client. The internal edges' audiences are per-service
# names that are not environment-specific, so they are defaulted in each service's own
# `appsettings.json` rather than being surfaced as deployment configuration -- DataServices, for
# instance, validates a SINGLE audience through `Authentication__Jwt__Audience` rather than a list.
# Note that shape difference: Gateway holds `ValidAudiences` (a list) and DataServices holds `Audience`
# (one value), so the two are not interchangeable keys even though they carry the same kind of name.
SECURITY_JWT_AUDIENCE=powerframework-gateway

# SECURITY_BASE_URL -- Security's in-network address, and the bearer AUTHORITY for all three verifiers.
#
# Consumed by:  all three verifiers as the bearer AUTHORITY, plus Gateway's and DataServices' upstream
#               address for the calls they make to Security:
#                 Gateway       ->  Authentication__Schemes__Bearer__Authority
#                                   and Gateway__Upstreams__Security
#                 DataServices  ->  Authentication__Jwt__Authority
#                                   and DataServices__Security__BaseAddress
#                 Persistence   ->  Jwt__Authority
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up
#
# THIS IS ALSO HOW EACH VERIFIER FINDS THE KEY SET, AND WHY NO JWKS VARIABLE EXISTS. There is no
# `SECURITY_JWKS_URL` in this roster and adding one would be dead configuration: nothing reads it. Each
# verifier derives the key-set address for itself from the authority above.
#   Gateway       the stock bearer handler fetches `<authority>/.well-known/openid-configuration` and
#                 follows its `jwks_uri`. Gateway exposes no key-set setting at all.
#   DataServices  the same discovery flow. `Authentication__Jwt__MetadataAddress` exists and overrides
#                 the DISCOVERY document address -- a different document from the key set -- and it is
#                 left unset so discovery resolves from the authority.
#   Persistence   the same discovery flow. `Jwt__MetadataAddress` exists and overrides the DISCOVERY
#                 document address, and it is left unset so discovery resolves from the authority.
#                 There is no key-set setting on this service either; an earlier revision declared a
#                 relative `Jwt__JwksPath` that nothing read, and it has been removed rather than left
#                 to look configurable.
# So a proxy that republishes Security on a different path is accommodated per service -- through
# DataServices' or Persistence's metadata address -- not through one shared absolute URL that none of
# the three actually consumes.
#
# ONE VARIABLE, THREE DIFFERENT SECTION NAMES -- AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE, NOT DRIFT. Gateway
# binds bearer settings at `Authentication:Schemes:Bearer`, the key ASP.NET Core's own handler reads,
# because Gateway configures the stock handler directly. DataServices binds at `Authentication:Jwt`
# because its validation surface is its own typed options. The Compose manifest therefore maps this one
# variable onto differently-named keys per service; it is recorded here so a reader does not "correct"
# one of them into the other.
#
# A plain in-network address. `security-service` is the Compose SERVICE NAME, resolved by Compose's
# own DNS on the orchestration network -- it is not a hostname that resolves anywhere else, which is
# why nothing outside the stack can use these values as-is.
#
# All three consumers hold VERIFICATION material only. Setting an authority does not confer any ability
# to mint; it tells the stock bearer handler where to fetch the public key set from.
#
# THE SCHEME IS https, AND THAT IS AN ENUMERATED DEVIATION RATHER THAN AN UNDOCUMENTED ONE (C-L).
#
# ⚠ AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS NOTE SAID `http`, AND ITS WARNING WAS INVERTED. It read "THE SCHEME IS
# http, BECAUSE THAT IS THE LISTENER THE ENVIRONMENT FIXES" and cautioned that "a mandatory-TLS listener
# would be an unapproved sixth deviation under which the documented bring-up could not start Security at
# all". The delivered listeners are HTTPS-ONLY, so the note described the opposite of what ships: an
# operator following it would have set a plaintext address that no listener answers, and read the
# resulting transport-layer failure as a broken key set rather than a wrong scheme. The value below has
# always been `https`; only this note was wrong.
#
# The deviation is real and is registered rather than waved through. The attached environment gates
# Security's readiness on `curl -sf http://localhost:5104/health` and supplies no certificate material,
# and this stack answers only over TLS -- so the corrected probe is
# `curl -sf --cacert <ca> https://localhost:5104/health`. It is enumerated as **D7** in
# `docs/BUILD.md` section 1.6, beside the two deviations that instruction interacts with, with the reason
# stated there: an authenticated transport on every newly created boundary is a constraint (C-G) the
# instruction's literal form cannot satisfy, so honouring the instruction verbatim would break the
# requirement it exists to serve. What would be undocumented -- and is not permitted -- is making the
# change and not registering it.
#
# CALLER AUTHENTICATION ON THE ISSUANCE EDGE DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE TRANSPORT. `POST /v1/tokens`
# authenticates its caller against Security's issuance roster, presented as an HTTP `Basic` credential
# -- the `clientCredential` scheme in `shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/OpenApi/security.v1.yaml`. A
# client certificate remains an accepted ALTERNATIVE wherever a deployment terminates TLS here, which is
# the mutual-TLS fallback AAP section 0.6.6.3 describes for that one pair. Either satisfies the
# operation; presenting neither is `401`. So the boundary is authenticated on the listener the
# environment actually binds (C-G), which is the property that matters, rather than on a listener that
# could not be brought up.
#
# WHAT PLAINTEXT COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED. This address is the system's trust bootstrap:
# everything downstream verifies against the key set published beneath it. Fetched in the clear that key
# set is substitutable on path -- an attacker who replaces it makes all three other services accept
# tokens the attacker signed, each behaving exactly as designed while doing it -- and the issuance
# credential and every bearer token are likewise capturable on path. That is ACCEPTED for the
# single-host, private-network bring-up the frozen environment describes, and it is documented here
# rather than left for a reader to work out.
#
# A DEPLOYMENT THAT WANTS TLS NEEDS NO CODE CHANGE. Point this variable, `SECURITY_JWT_ISSUER` and the
# probe URLs at the `https` address, supply the transport-security variables in section 5, and add
# `ClientCertificateMode` `AllowCertificate` to Security's endpoint. Nothing in this repository
# restricts the scheme, and `security.v1.yaml` records the same position on its single server entry.
SECURITY_BASE_URL=https://security-service:5104


####################################################################################################
# 2. PERSISTENCE -- `persistence-service`, TWO TLS listeners: `https://+:5101` `Http1` for its REST
#    surface, which is the readiness address the environment documents, and `https://+:5111` `Http2` for
#    its gRPC contracts C-05..C-08. THE ONLY SERVICE WITH A STORAGE PROVIDER. Why two endpoints rather
#    than one is explained in full at PERSISTENCE_GRPC_URL in section 3.
#
# SQLITE IS THE ONLY DATABASE PROVISIONED, AND THAT IS A DISCOVERY OUTCOME RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
# It is the ONLY evidenced storage binding in the whole repository: one connection call, one URI grammar,
# and exactly one piece of DDL -- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS COMPANY` at
# [ws_objects/pfw.tests.pbl.src/w_test_sqlite.srw:L463-L469], declared column for column as
# `ID INTEGER PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL`, `NAME TEXT NOT NULL`, `AGE INT NOT NULL`, `ADDRESS CHAR(50)`,
# `SALARY REAL`, `BIRTH TEXT`. That single table is the schema this service provisions, and the
# DataWindow that updates it is the golden-master fixture for the whole retrieval/validation/update
# triple.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT DDL IS OFTEN READ AS SAYING AND DOES NOT SAY, both corrected here because a port that
# believes either of them diverges from the oracle on the first row (C-K):
#   * `ID` IS NOT AN `AUTOINCREMENT` COLUMN. The keyword does not appear; the legacy source's own comment
#     beside it reads 自增列, "auto-increment column", which is the INTENT rather than the declaration.
#     `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` makes the column an ALIAS FOR THE ROWID, so SQLite assigns a value when none
#     is supplied -- but by the largest-existing-rowid-plus-one rule, which REUSES the values of deleted
#     rows. `AUTOINCREMENT` is the keyword that forbids reuse, via the `sqlite_sequence` table, and it is
#     absent. The identity round trip must therefore not assume monotonicity.
#   * `CHAR(50)` IS NOT A WIDTH CONSTRAINT. SQLite has no fixed-width string type: the declared type
#     merely determines AFFINITY, and `CHAR(50)` carries TEXT affinity with the length ignored entirely.
#     A 200-character address is stored in full, unpadded and untruncated. `REAL` likewise gives REAL
#     affinity rather than a fixed scale, and `TEXT` on `BIRTH` gives no date semantics at all. The
#     DataWindow declares `char(100)`, `char(200)`, `decimal(2)` and `date` against those four columns --
#     four preserved mismatches, catalogued with their consequences in docs/PARITY.md section 3.5.
#
# The legacy transaction layer declares only two database types,
# SQL Server as 0 and Oracle as 1 -- and NEITHER has a schema, a connection string or any DDL anywhere
# in the estate. Their dialect-specific behaviour survives as PAGING REWRITERS that are pure string
# transforms (statement plus page size and index in, statement out), so both are reproduced and
# unit-tested with no instance of either engine in existence.
#
# Consequently there is NO SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL or MySQL variable in this file: no server
# name, no port, no database name, no account, no password. Inventing one would fabricate a database
# the legacy never had.
####################################################################################################

# PERSISTENCE_SQLITE_DATA_DIRECTORY -- the directory the database file lives in.
#
# Consumed by:  Persistence only  ->  Sqlite__DataDirectory
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up
#
# THE URI IS COMPOSED, NOT CONFIGURED, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS A DIRECTORY RATHER THAN A URI. Persistence
# holds the legacy URI's parts as five discrete settings under a root `Sqlite` section -- data directory,
# database file name, mode, check and journal -- and assembles the connection URI from them. There is no
# `Sqlite:Uri` setting to hand a pre-assembled string to; a variable pointing at one would be dead
# configuration. Only the directory is templated here, because it is the ONE part that is deployment
# topology: it is the volume mount point, and it is the only part that differs between a container and a
# developer's loopback run.
#
# THE OTHER FOUR PARTS ARE PRESERVED LEGACY DEFAULTS AND ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TEMPLATED. Each is declared
# in Persistence's own `appsettings.json`, so templating it here could only either restate the same value
# -- noise -- or override a preserved default from orchestration, which is the silent behavioural change
# the migration forbids. They are listed so the knobs are known, with the environment spelling of each:
#   Sqlite__DatabaseFileName   `test.db`, the legacy file name, retained so stored comparisons resolve
#   Sqlite__Mode               `rwc` -- read, write, create
#   Sqlite__Check              UNSET, meaning no integrity check on open. It is three-state: unset, a
#                              full check, or a quick one
#   Sqlite__Journal            `DELETE`
#
# THE GRAMMAR THOSE PARTS REPRODUCE, FROM THE ONE CALL SITE IN THE ESTATE
#   [ws_objects/pfw.tests.pbl.src/w_test_sqlite.srw:L456]  Open("test.db?mode=rwc"/*[,password]*/)
#   [:L452] cites the SQLite URI specification, so URI conventions apply and further parameters are
#           appended with `&`.
#   [:L454] `check[=quick]`   integrity check on open. A bare `check` is a full `PRAGMA integrity_check`;
#           `check=quick` selects `PRAGMA quick_check` instead.
#   [:L455] `journal[=DELETE|TRUNCATE|PERSIST|MEMORY|WAL|OFF]`   journal mode. Legacy DEFAULT is
#           `DELETE` -- note that this is NOT the `WAL` a modern reader might assume, and the legacy
#           default is what is preserved.
#
#   So the assembled shape is:  <directory>/<file>?mode=<mode>[&check[=quick]][&journal[=<mode>]]
#
# THERE IS NO PASSWORD VARIABLE IN THIS ROSTER, AND THERE WILL NOT BE ONE IN THIS PHASE.
# Persistence does carry a `Sqlite__Password` setting and it is environment-only -- it appears in no
# settings file, by design, because an assembled URI carrying it would be a credential. It is not
# templated here, not even empty, and that is the same reasoning section 6.2 applies to an unowned
# secret: a declared credential variable for a path with no parity invites populating it.
# The `/*[,password]*/` shown inline at `:L456` sits in the second-argument position of the two-overload
# open surface at `ws_objects/pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl.src/n_sqlite.sru:L17-L18`. Encrypted-SQLite parity
# is OUT OF PHASE-1 SCOPE: the cipher-enabled library shipped in this repository is materially older
# than the plain one, and its key-derivation and per-page integrity options are not reachable through
# any framework API, so there is no evidence of the settings an existing encrypted file was created
# with. Since the target provider tracks a current SQLite, page-format parity for such a file cannot be
# reproduced, and this is recorded as a known limitation rather than silently attempted
# (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md section 8.3). If that setting is ever populated, the ASSEMBLED URI becomes a
# credential in its own right -- never logged, never returned in an error payload, never captured into a
# characterization recording (docs/SECRETS.md section 5.2).
#
# WHY AN ABSOLUTE PATH AND NOT A RELATIVE ONE
# The directory is the mount point of the `persistence-db` Docker volume, which is attached to
# Persistence alone. That makes the file survive container recreation, which the paired-capture rule
# requires: for one workflow ID, the legacy-side and target-side recordings must be taken against the SAME
# `persistence-db` volume state, and the volume must not be recreated or reseeded between them, or the
# two recordings are not comparable at all. The volume must be writable by the image's non-root user.
# Persistence's `appsettings.Development.json` overrides this to a temp path for a loopback run, which is
# the one place the two topologies legitimately differ.
#
# The legacy file name is retained so stored comparisons keep resolving. What is NOT reproduced is the
# `FileDelete("test.db")` that precedes the open at `:L450`: that is a test-harness action, and
# reproducing it on service start would destroy the very volume state the paired-capture rule depends on.
PERSISTENCE_SQLITE_DATA_DIRECTORY=/var/lib/powerframework

# PERSISTENCE_TRANSPOOL_KEEPALIVE -- keep pooled transaction objects alive when their refcount hits zero.
#
# Consumed by:  Persistence only  ->  TransactionPool__KeepAlive
# Mandatory:    no
# Default:      false, which is the LEGACY default and not a conservative choice of ours. The legacy
#               gates the entire keep-alive path on `$SQL.TransPool.KeepAlive`
#               [ws_objects/pfw.thread.ext.pbl.src/n_cst_thread_trans_pool.sru:L76], and with the
#               setting absent the boolean is false, so nothing is kept alive and the idle-collection
#               handler is never even subscribed [:L80].
PERSISTENCE_TRANSPOOL_KEEPALIVE=false

# PERSISTENCE_TRANSPOOL_KEEPALIVE_EXPIRE_SECONDS -- idle lifetime of a kept-alive pooled transaction.
#
# Consumed by:  Persistence only  ->  TransactionPool__KeepAliveExpireSeconds
# Mandatory:    no
# Default:      30 seconds, which is 30000 ms -- the legacy `KEEPALIVE_EXPIRE` constant verbatim
#               [n_cst_thread_trans_pool.sru:L53].
#
# THIS LINE AND PERSISTENCE'S OWN DEFAULT ARE THE SAME EFFECTIVE VALUE, STATED TWO WAYS. Persistence's
# `appsettings.json` declares 0 -- the value the legacy sees when the setting is ABSENT -- and 0 falls
# back to 30000 ms by the rule below. Writing 30 here states that same 30000 ms explicitly, which is what
# makes the seconds unit legible at the point an operator would change it. It is not an override: no
# observable behaviour differs between the two spellings.
#
# THE UNIT IS SECONDS, AND THAT IS A PRESERVED LEGACY QUIRK WORTH KNOWING ABOUT. The legacy reads
# `$SQL.TransPool.KeepAliveExpireTime` and MULTIPLIES IT BY 1000 [:L78], so the setting is in seconds
# while the constant it defaults to, and every internal comparison, is in milliseconds. Both halves are
# preserved: this variable is in seconds, and the conversion happens on the way in.
#
# Also preserved: a configured value that yields a non-positive millisecond result FALLS BACK to 30000 ms
# rather than being rejected or meaning "never expire" [:L79]. So 0 and any negative value both behave
# as 30 seconds. That is the legacy behaviour, not a validation gap to be tidied.
#
# Expiry is measured on the process CPU clock -- the idle start is stamped at [:L97] and compared at
# [:L215] -- so it is one of the non-determinism sources seamed for substitution, and characterization
# runs drive it with a deterministic clock rather than depending on wall time.
PERSISTENCE_TRANSPOOL_KEEPALIVE_EXPIRE_SECONDS=30


####################################################################################################
# 3. DATASERVICES -- `dataservices-service`, TWO TLS listeners: `https://+:5102` `Http1` for the
#    documented readiness probe, `/v1/ping` and the thin REST projection consumed only by Gateway, and
#    `https://+:5112` `Http2` for its gRPC contracts C-03 and C-04. gRPC is its primary transport. It
#    splits its endpoints the same way Persistence does and for the same reason -- explained in full at
#    PERSISTENCE_GRPC_URL below.
#
# THE CALL GRAPH IS LAYERED AND ACYCLIC, AND THE ROSTER BELOW ENCODES IT RATHER THAN DESCRIBING IT:
#   Gateway      -> DataServices, Security
#   DataServices -> Persistence, Security
#   Persistence  -> Security's published keys only
#   Gateway      <- external clients, and nothing else in the system
#
# Read the negatives, because they are the part that is enforced: Gateway has NO Persistence address --
# configuring one would put SQL generation one hop from the ingress -- and nothing reaches DataServices
# except Gateway, nor Persistence except DataServices.
####################################################################################################

# PERSISTENCE_GRPC_URL -- Persistence's in-network gRPC address, on its HTTP/2 endpoint.
#
# Consumed by:  DataServices only  ->  DataServices__Persistence__Address
# Mandatory:    YES in effect. The address carries `[Required]` and defaults to EMPTY in code, because a
#               code default could only ever be one topology's and a service that configured nothing
#               would then look usable while pointing nowhere. DataServices cannot serve a retrieval or
#               an update without reaching Persistence, so a missing value fails validation at startup.
#
# An in-network address; `persistence-service` is the Compose service name, resolved by Compose's own
# DNS on the orchestration network.
#
# TWO ENDPOINTS, ONE PER PROTOCOL VERSION -- MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Persistence serves two
# kinds of traffic that do not share a protocol version: contracts C-05 through C-08 are gRPC and
# REQUIRE HTTP/2, while `/health` and `/v1/ping` are probed with HTTP/1.1 by the readiness gate whose
# success gates Gateway (C-L) and by the end-to-end suite. Measured directly on SDK 10.0.302:
#   CLEARTEXT, Protocols "Http1AndHttp2"  ->  HTTP/1.1 GET /health returns 200, but HTTP/2 is DISABLED
#                                             OUTRIGHT and Kestrel says so at startup: "HTTP/2 is not
#                                             enabled ... TLS is not enabled ... Connections to this
#                                             endpoint will use HTTP/1.1". An h2c prior-knowledge
#                                             request fails with "Remote peer returned unexpected data
#                                             while we expected SETTINGS frame". Every gRPC call is lost.
#   CLEARTEXT, Protocols "Http2"          ->  gRPC works, but an HTTP/1.1 `GET /health` returns 400, so
#                                             the readiness gate could never satisfy and Gateway would
#                                             never report healthy.
#   TLS, Protocols "Http1AndHttp2"        ->  BOTH work on ONE port, because HTTP/2 negotiation is an
#                                             ALPN feature of the TLS handshake.
#
# THE THIRD ROW IS WHAT THIS STACK RUNS, AND THE TWO PORTS ARE KEPT ANYWAY. Under TLS one port could
# carry both, so the split is a choice rather than a necessity -- and the reason is misaddressing. Each
# endpoint pins ONE protocol version, so a readiness probe and a gRPC channel each address a listener
# that can only answer the thing it is for: a gRPC channel aimed at the HTTP/1.1 port fails with
# HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED before the request arrives, and an HTTP/1.1 probe aimed at the HTTP/2 port gets a
# 400. Under `Http1AndHttp2` both mistakes would instead succeed at the transport layer and fail later,
# somewhere less obviously attributable. Dropping gRPC is not available either: AAP section 0.1.5 decides the
# transport per service and C-05..C-08 are gRPC.
#
# WHAT THE SECOND PORT COSTS, AND WHY IT COSTS NOTHING THE ENVIRONMENT FIXES. The environment documents
# `/health` addresses on 5101-5105 and documents NO gRPC address anywhere -- so 5101 keeps exactly the
# meaning it was given, and 5111 adds an address the environment never named. Nothing is moved, nothing
# is reassigned, and 5103 stays unallocated for the deferred DesignSystem service. The cost is that
# Persistence and DataServices each have TWO addresses a caller must keep straight, which is why the
# variables are named for what they carry: `_GRPC_URL` for the HTTP/2 endpoint a SERVICE dials, and
# `_BASE_URL` for the HTTP/1.1 endpoint a PROBE and the end-to-end suite read. Security and Gateway
# publish no gRPC contract and so have one endpoint and one variable each.
#
# A gRPC channel pointed at the HTTP/1.1 endpoint fails every call with `HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED` before the
# request arrives, and a probe pointed at the HTTP/2 endpoint gets a 400 -- so the two variables are
# not interchangeable and swapping them fails loudly rather than subtly.
#
# A DEPLOYMENT THAT TERMINATES TLS MAY COLLAPSE THEM BACK TO ONE. Supply the transport-security
# variables in section 5, declare a single `Http1AndHttp2` endpoint, and point both variables at it; the
# third measured row above is then in force. No code change is required for that, and nothing in this
# repository restricts the scheme.
PERSISTENCE_GRPC_URL=https://persistence-service:5111

# PERSISTENCE_BASE_URL -- Persistence's in-network REST address, on its HTTP/1.1 endpoint. This is the
# address the readiness gate probes. The attached environment writes that probe as
# `curl -sf http://localhost:5101/health`; this listener is HTTPS-ONLY, so the form that works is
# `curl -sf --cacert <ca> https://localhost:5101/health` -- the same shape with the scheme corrected and
# the local anchor trusted. The transport departure is enumerated as **D7** in `docs/BUILD.md`
# section 1.6 (C-L, C-K); the port, the path and the anonymity of the probe are unchanged.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway  ->  Gateway__HealthProbes__Persistence, via
#               GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_PERSISTENCE_URL in section 4, which defaults to this same listener.
#               No service dials it for a contract call: `/health` is anonymous and `/v1/ping` exists to
#               prove the boundary is guarded, and neither is a business operation.
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up.
PERSISTENCE_BASE_URL=https://persistence-service:5101


####################################################################################################
# 4. GATEWAY -- `gateway-service`, ONE TLS listener, `https://+:5105`, `Http1`. REST + OpenAPI.
#    THE SOLE INGRESS AND COMPOSITION ROOT.
#
#    NO INGRESS ADDRESS VARIABLE EXISTS IN THIS ROSTER, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
#    Gateway declares its own endpoint in `appsettings.json`, so the documented access URL is bound by
#    the repository rather than by whatever brings it up. An earlier revision left the address to this
#    layer; nothing then bound 5105 at all, because there is no manifest yet and no `ASPNETCORE_URLS`
#    here, so a plain `dotnet run` bound Kestrel's own default of 5000 and the composition root was
#    unreachable at the one address every document names. An operator who genuinely needs to move it
#    still can: `ASPNETCORE_URLS` and `Kestrel__Endpoints__Default__Url` both take precedence over a
#    settings file, and neither needs declaring here to work.
#
# Reachable at https://localhost:5105 once the stack is up. `/health` is anonymous on all four services;
# `/v1/ping` requires a token on all four and returns 401 without one, which is the standing proof that
# every newly created boundary is authenticated rather than merely documented as such.
#
# Gateway reports healthy only AFTER Persistence, DataServices and Security do -- the Compose manifest
# expresses that with `depends_on` using a health condition, so the readiness gate opens in dependency
# order.
####################################################################################################

# DATASERVICES_GRPC_URL -- DataServices' gRPC address, as Gateway's gRPC channel reaches it.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway only  ->  Gateway__Upstreams__DataServices
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up
#
# An in-network address; `dataservices-service` is the Compose service name. DataServices' primary
# transport is gRPC -- contracts C-03 and C-04 -- so this address names its `Http2` endpoint on 5112.
# It splits its endpoints the same way Persistence does, for the misaddressing reason explained in full
# at PERSISTENCE_GRPC_URL above: one endpoint per protocol version, so neither can be reached by the
# wrong kind of caller.
DATASERVICES_GRPC_URL=https://dataservices-service:5112

# DATASERVICES_BASE_URL -- DataServices' in-network REST address, on its HTTP/1.1 endpoint. It carries
# the anonymous `/health` the readiness gate probes, `/v1/ping`, and the thin REST projection consumed
# only by Gateway.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway  ->  Gateway__HealthProbes__DataServices, via
#               GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_DATASERVICES_URL in this section, which defaults to this same
#               listener.
# Mandatory:    no -- this default works for the local bring-up.
DATASERVICES_BASE_URL=https://dataservices-service:5102

# GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_PERSISTENCE_URL / _DATASERVICES_URL / _SECURITY_URL -- the three addresses
# Gateway PROBES for readiness, which is not the same list as the two it CALLS.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway only  ->  Gateway__HealthProbes__Persistence
#                                 Gateway__HealthProbes__DataServices
#                                 Gateway__HealthProbes__Security
# Mandatory:    all three, or the published aggregate cannot be produced at all.
# Default:      the in-network addresses below, which are exactly the three `_BASE_URL` listeners above.
#               A probe speaks HTTP/1.1, so it reads the HTTP/1.1 endpoint of each service and never the
#               `_GRPC_URL` one -- an HTTP/1.1 `GET /health` against an `Http2` endpoint answers 400, and
#               the readiness gate would never open.
#
# THREE, WHERE THE CALL ROSTER HAS TWO, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE CONTRACT. `gateway.v1.yaml` bounds
# `AggregateHealthReport.upstreams` at exactly three items and closes the member name over
# `persistence`, `dataservices` and `security`, so a two-member probe group leaves C-10 unbuildable.
# Gateway nonetheless holds NO Persistence client, channel or generated stub: this value authorises
# exactly one anonymous `GET /health` and nothing further, which is how C-10 and the layering rule that
# only DataServices calls Persistence are satisfied at the same time.
#
# DO NOT FOLD THESE ONTO THE BASE URLS ABOVE, even though the values coincide. The base URLs are what a
# CALLER dials; these are what an OBSERVER reads. Sharing one variable would make a Persistence call
# address arrive in Gateway's configuration looking like a probe, which is the one topology breach the
# separation exists to prevent -- and the two option groups are separate types for the same reason.
#
# All three probes are `https`, because all four listeners terminate TLS, and each therefore needs the
# trust anchor INTERNAL_TLS_TRUSTED_CA_PATH names -- the certificate they present is issued by the local
# authority of docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 9.3.1, which is in no container's OS trust store. Nothing here
# restricts the scheme, so a deployment fronting these services with something else can point all three
# elsewhere.
GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_PERSISTENCE_URL=https://persistence-service:5101
GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_DATASERVICES_URL=https://dataservices-service:5102
GATEWAY_HEALTH_PROBE_SECURITY_URL=https://security-service:5104

# GATEWAY_LOCALE -- the default locale the composition root installs.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway only  ->  Gateway__Locale
# Mandatory:    no
# Default:      en
#
# THIS DEFAULT REPRODUCES A LEGACY DEFECT ON PURPOSE. The legacy application's open event initializes
# the framework and then sets the language with a bare literal assignment:
#
#   [ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L91]       pfwInitialize(Enums.INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_ALL)
#   [ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L94]       lang = "en"
#   [ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L95-L102]  choose case lang -> n_cst_i18n_en / _chs / _cht
#   [ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L103]      I18N(locale)
#
# THE DEFECT IS THE HARDCODING, NOT THE VALUE. So the value `en` is preserved as the DEFAULT -- an
# observer sees the same locale the legacy selects -- while being made overridable here, which removes
# the un-configurability without changing the observable default. Correcting the value instead would be
# the silent behavioural change the migration forbids.
#
# The three providers the legacy selects between are `en`, `chs` and `cht`. `chs` is the base locale and
# its provider is a genuine no-op. Nothing validates this value on Gateway: an unrecognised locale falls
# through the provider selection and localization then passes text through unchanged -- silently, with no
# log and no untranslated marker. That silent-passthrough fallback is also preserved legacy behaviour.
#
# GATEWAY IS THE ONLY SERVICE WHOSE LOCALE IS TEMPLATED HERE, AND THAT IS ON PURPOSE. Gateway is the
# composition root that installs the provider, reproducing `I18N(locale)` at `pfw.sra:L103`. DataServices
# carries its own locale for the messages it raises from inside its validation path, defaulted to the
# same `en` from the same locator, and it is NOT surfaced here because its two settings do not behave the
# same way: DataServices REJECTS anything outside `en`, `chs`, `cht` at startup, case-sensitively, and
# rejects blank as well, whereas Gateway accepts any string. If you change the locale, decide about both
# -- a value Gateway accepts silently may stop DataServices from starting at all.
GATEWAY_LOCALE=en

# GATEWAY_CAPABILITY_FLAGS -- the eight-bit capability gate, as a decimal bitmask.
#
# Consumed by:  Gateway only  ->  Gateway__CapabilityFlags
# Mandatory:    no
# Default:      3847 -- `INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_ALL`, the value the legacy application requests, and the same
#               value Gateway's own `appsettings.json` already carries. This line states the code default
#               rather than overriding it. Reasoning below.
#
# THE BIT TABLE, VERBATIM FROM [ws_objects/pfw.shared.pbl.src/enums.sru:L41-L48], under that file's own
# comment `//Initialize flags (pfwInitialize:[flags])` at [:L40]:
#
#     1  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_UI          [:L41]  ->  DesignSystem   (DEFERRED)
#     2  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_SCITER      [:L42]  ->  ScriptBridge   (DEFERRED)
#     4  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_BLINK       [:L43]  ->  ScriptBridge   (DEFERRED)
#     8  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_BLINKFAST   [:L44]  ->  ScriptBridge   (DEFERRED)
#   256  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_ORCA        [:L45]  ->  packaging tooling, not a service at all
#   512  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_SQLITE      [:L46]  ->  Persistence -- THE ONLY BIT WITH AN IN-SCOPE CONSUMER
#  1024  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_DPIAWARE    [:L47]  ->  DesignSystem   (DEFERRED)
#  2048  INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_WEBVIEW     [:L48]  ->  ScriptBridge   (DEFERRED)
#
# The sequence is sparse on purpose -- it runs 1, 2, 4, 8 and then jumps to 256, skipping 16 through 128
# -- and the gap is the legacy's own. Renumbering densely would look tidier and would invalidate every
# stored configuration value and every characterization comparison.
#
# `INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_ALL` at [:L49] sums SEVEN of the eight bits and evaluates to 3847, NOT 3855: it
# OMITS `INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_BLINKFAST`. That looks like an oversight and is not -- blink and blinkfast are
# alternative builds of ONE engine, so an "everything on" constant enabling both would be incoherent. The
# omission is reproduced exactly, and 3847 remains the code-side and `appsettings.json` default because
# it is what the legacy application requests at `pfw.sra:L91`.
#
# WHY THE ORCHESTRATION VALUE IS 3847 AND NOT A NARROWER MASK. Because 3847 is what the legacy requests,
# and orchestration is not the place to change a preserved behavioural default. Gateway's own
# `appsettings.json` declares 3847 and its options type defaults to `INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_ALL` by consuming
# the constant rather than restating a literal, for exactly this reason. A deployment-time override to
# anything else would make the running system disagree with the code default and with `pfw.sra:L91` --
# the silent behavioural change the migration forbids -- so this line RESTATES the default so an operator
# can see the knob, and does not move it.
#
# AN EARLIER FORM OF THIS FILE SET 512, THE SQLITE BIT ALONE. The argument was that seven of the eight
# bits name capability owned by the four deferred services, and that the read-only legacy documentation
# warns a requested module whose native library is absent makes initialization FAIL rather than degrade
# [docs/README.md:L26, :L30]. Both halves of that are true of the LEGACY and neither transfers here, which
# is what made 512 wrong:
#
#   * REQUESTING A BIT IS NOT IMPLEMENTING THE CAPABILITY BEHIND IT. Gateway's capability gate is a pure
#     function of the mask. It probes for no native library, resolves no path, and holds no client,
#     handler, placeholder or exception-throwing shell for any deferred capability. The four deferred
#     services surface only as reserved Gateway routes returning `501 Not Implemented`, and the mask does
#     not change that either way. So 3847 requests nothing this phase has not built.
#   * THE FAILURE MODE THE WARNING DESCRIBES CANNOT OCCUR. It is a native-DLL-resolution failure in the
#     PowerBuilder runtime. This migration eliminates that runtime: there is no `sciter.dll`, no
#     `blink.dll` and no `pfw.dll` on the path, and nothing in the composition root loads one. There is
#     no library to be absent, so no bit can fail initialization on its account.
#
# SQLITE REMAINS THE ONLY BIT WITH AN IN-SCOPE CONSUMER, and that consumer is Persistence rather than
# Gateway -- so Gateway reports that bit and takes no action on it either. That fact is preserved as
# DOCUMENTATION, which is what the bit table above is, rather than being enforced by narrowing the mask.
# Narrowing enforced nothing anyway; it only diverged the deployed value from the legacy one.
#
# To request a different set, bitwise-OR the bits together and write the decimal sum -- 3847 for the
# legacy all-capabilities request, or 512 for the SQLite bit alone if a deployment has a reason to state
# that explicitly. The value is NOT range-checked and is projected unsigned, so a negative number wraps
# rather than being rejected, and unrecognised bits -- the unassigned 16, 32, 64 and 128, and anything
# above 2048 -- are reported as data rather than refused. Both are preserved behaviours, not validation
# gaps to be tidied.
GATEWAY_CAPABILITY_FLAGS=3847


####################################################################################################
# 5. SHARED -- applies to all four services
####################################################################################################

# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT -- selects which `appsettings.<Environment>.json` overrides load.
#
# Consumed by:  all four services
# Mandatory:    no
# Default:      Development
#
# WHAT THIS VARIABLE DOES, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. It selects an overlay, and the overlays in this
# repository change HOSTS, log levels and developer-ergonomic idle windows. It is NOT a security switch:
# no overlay turns a check off that its base file turns on, and no token-validation check is
# configurable from either file at all -- issuer, audience, lifetime and signing-key validation are
# unconditional in code precisely so that no settings file can relax one.
#
# WHAT DOES DIFFER BETWEEN THE TWO, AND WHY IT IS NOT A RELAXATION. `RequireHttpsMetadata` is TRUE in
# every service's settings that declares it, base and overlay alike, because every authority in this
# repository is `https`. The setting names a fact about the authority's address rather than a hardening
# level: set true against an `http` authority it does not make the fetch secure, it makes the fetch FAIL,
# and the service then rejects every token because it never obtained a key set. That is why the coherence
# test in shared/PowerFramework.Contracts.Tests asserts the two can never disagree inside one file, in
# either direction: a settings file may not require secure metadata retrieval from a plaintext authority,
# and may not waive it for a secure one.
#
# The confinement principle still holds for the one thing an overlay genuinely moves: a change in a base
# file is inherited by every environment that forgets to override it, whereas one in the Development file
# applies only when this variable says `Development`. That is why the overlays carry only host swaps.
#
# WHAT PLAINTEXT COSTS is stated in full at SECURITY_BASE_URL rather than glossed here, together with
# what a deployment does instead if it wants TLS -- which needs no code change.
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development
# Deployed form:
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production

# TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH -- a server certificate, for a deployment that
# chooses to terminate TLS at these listeners.
#
# Consumed by:  any service whose endpoint is declared `https`  ->  Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path
#                                                                  Kestrel__Certificates__Default__KeyPath
# Mandatory:    YES in effect. All four listeners declare `https` endpoints, and Kestrel refuses to start
#               an HTTPS endpoint whose certificate it cannot resolve -- see the fail-fast note below.
# Default:      NONE, deliberately. There is no certificate and no private key in this repository and
#               there never may be (C-F); a default here could only ever be a path to material that
#               does not exist, or worse, material that does.
#
# WHY THESE EXIST AND WHAT DEPENDS ON THEM. They are the server half of every listener in the stack, and
# nothing starts without them: the reasons are the exposures recorded at SECURITY_BASE_URL -- the key set
# is the system's trust bootstrap and is substitutable on path when fetched in the clear, and the issuance
# credential and every bearer token would otherwise be capturable. Recording the variables here rather
# than the material is what keeps that a configuration concern with no secret in the repository (C-F).
#
# HOW KESTREL RESOLVES THE MATERIAL, AND WHY ABSENCE IS FAIL-FAST RATHER THAN A DOWNGRADE. For an HTTPS
# endpoint Kestrel looks for a certificate in a fixed order: one declared on the endpoint itself, then
# `Kestrel:Certificates:Default`, then the local ASP.NET Core development certificate -- and if none is
# found IT REFUSES TO START. It never falls back to plaintext. So an endpoint switched to `https` with
# these variables unset fails loudly at boot rather than quietly serving something unencrypted.
#
# WHAT TO SET THEM TO. Paths to a PEM certificate chain and its PEM private key, MOUNTED READ-ONLY into
# each container from the orchestration secret layer -- for example a Docker or Kubernetes secret
# projected at /run/secrets. Neither file is built into an image and neither is committed here. The PEM
# pair is preferred over a PKCS#12 bundle precisely because it needs no passphrase, so this file acquires
# no passphrase variable to protect.
#
# THE CERTIFICATE MUST CARRY SUBJECT ALTERNATIVE NAMES FOR EVERY ORIGIN IT IS PRESENTED UNDER, and this
# is the one requirement on it that is easy to get wrong and silent when wrong. ONE pair serves all three
# TLS listeners, so the same certificate is presented as `persistence-service`, `dataservices-service`
# and `security-service` inside a Compose network and as `localhost` from the host during a loopback run
# or an end-to-end suite. Current TLS stacks -- including the one .NET uses -- IGNORE the common name for
# host matching and read `subjectAltName` only, so a certificate carrying `CN=security-service` and no
# SAN extension matches NONE of those four names, including the one it appears to carry. An earlier
# revision of the generation recipe in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 9.3.1 issued exactly that certificate; the
# recipe now emits `DNS:persistence-service, DNS:dataservices-service, DNS:security-service,
# DNS:gateway-service, DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1, IP:::1` and verifies each name before deploying.
# Per-service certificates are equally correct: issue one per service carrying that service's DNS name
# plus the loopback entries, and point each container's pair at its own material rather than at a shared
# one.
#
# A DEVELOPER RUNNING SERVICES DIRECTLY NEEDS NEITHER VARIABLE. The development-certificate fallback
# above covers a loopback run once `dotnet dev-certs https --trust` has installed it. The end-to-end
# suite keeps certificate validation ON (`ignoreHTTPSErrors` is false), so an untrusted certificate
# surfaces as a real finding about the stack rather than being suppressed.
TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH=
TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH=


####################################################################################################
# 6. DELIBERATE OMISSIONS -- WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT
#
# Every entry below is a variable a reader might reasonably expect to find. Each is absent for a stated
# reason, recorded here so nobody concludes it was forgotten and helpfully adds it back.
#
# 6.1  NO PER-SERVICE SIGNING KEY, UNDER ANY SPELLING.
#      There is no `GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNING_KEY`, `DATASERVICES_JWT_SIGNING_KEY` or
#      `PERSISTENCE_JWT_SIGNING_KEY`. Security is the sole issuer; the other three hold verification
#      material only. A per-service signing key would make each of them a second issuer, and the
#      security properties of a sole-issuer topology depend on there being exactly one. The attached
#      environment described five independent per-service signing secrets against a placeholder service
#      roster; that model is SUPERSEDED here, and the supersession is documented rather than silently
#      absorbed (docs/SECRETS.md sections 4.2 and 4.4).
#
# 6.2  NOTHING AT ALL FOR THE FOUR DEFERRED SERVICES.
#      DesignSystem, Documents, Integration and ScriptBridge receive NO signing variable, NO verification
#      variable, NO mutual-TLS variable and NO address. They are not built in this phase -- not partially,
#      and not as a stub -- so there is nothing for a variable to configure. In particular the attached
#      environment's `design-service` and `localization-service` signing-key names are deliberately NOT
#      provisioned: DesignSystem is precisely one of the four deferred services, and localization is a
#      shared LIBRARY rather than a service at all. The reason is not tidiness. Provisioning a credential
#      for a service that does not exist creates an UNOWNED SECRET -- one that nothing consumes, nothing
#      rotates and nobody is accountable for, but which is nonetheless real and must still be protected.
#      That is how credential sprawl starts (docs/SECRETS.md section 4.4). Port 5103 is likewise left as a
#      commented-out slot in the Compose manifest rather than reassigned, so the shape of the eventual
#      system stays legible; the four deferred capabilities surface only as reserved Gateway routes that
#      return `501 Not Implemented`, which is routing metadata and not a stub of anything.
#
# 6.3  NO CERTIFICATE MATERIAL IS PROVISIONED FOR THE MUTUAL-TLS PAIR -- NEITHER HALF -- AND THAT IS A
#      CONSEQUENCE OF THE FROZEN LISTENER CONTRACT RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
#      Mutual TLS is the documented fallback for a pair where a token issuer is inappropriate, and it
#      applies to that pair ONLY. The rule has exactly one instance: `POST /v1/tokens`, which cannot be
#      protected by a bearer token because a caller cannot present a token in order to obtain its first
#      one. The published contract therefore accepts EITHER of two caller credentials there -- an HTTP
#      `Basic` credential naming a subject on Security's issuance roster, or a client certificate -- and
#      a request presenting neither is refused with `401`.
#      THE `Basic` SCHEME IS THE PRIMARY ONE, because the operation reads the header itself and so works
#      wherever the handshake is terminated. So issuance is authenticated on every topology, which is what
#      C-G requires, with the client certificate as the stronger alternative rather than the only path.
#      NEITHER CERTIFICATE HALF IS PRE-FILLED. The variables exist -- SECURITY_MTLS_CLIENT_CA_PATH for
#      the trust anchor, and the two per-caller pairs for Gateway and DataServices, all in section 1 --
#      and all of them default to EMPTY. That is load bearing rather than tidy: Gateway and DataServices
#      load their pair eagerly at startup and turn a missing file into a fail-fast error, so a default
#      naming a mount point no manifest provides would stop the documented bring-up dead. Empty means
#      "this deployment presents no client certificate", which is a supported state.
#      WHAT A DEPLOYMENT ADDS TO ADOPT IT, in one edit and with no code change: the server certificate
#      through TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and TLS_CERTIFICATE_KEY_PATH in section 5, `ClientCertificateMode`
#      `AllowCertificate` on Security's endpoint, the trust anchor, and one client pair per calling
#      participant. `AllowCertificate` rather than `RequireCertificate` is deliberate and was measured:
#      RequireCertificate aborts the TLS handshake for a client presenting none, which breaks the
#      ANONYMOUS `/health` probe and with it the readiness chain that gates Gateway. AllowCertificate
#      requests the certificate and hands it to the application, so `POST /v1/tokens` validates it per
#      operation while `/health`, the JWK set and the discovery document stay anonymously reachable.
#      Two operational consequences hold either way. Any such path points at material MOUNTED from the
#      orchestration secret layer -- no certificate and no private key is committed to this repository or
#      embedded in an image. And the issuance path must NOT sit behind a TLS-terminating proxy: an
#      intermediary that terminates TLS there either discards the client certificate or leaves Security
#      trusting a forwarded assertion of an identity it cannot verify, and both outcomes defeat the
#      sole-issuer topology.
#
# 6.4  NO CREDENTIALED CONNECTION STRING, AND NO DATABASE ACCOUNT OF ANY KIND.
#      The legacy transaction descriptor carries nine fields -- dbms, servername, database, logid,
#      logpass, dbparm, lock, autocommit and userparm
#      [ws_objects/pfw.thread.ext.pbl.src/transactiondata.srs:L4-L12] -- and the legacy accessor moves
#      `logpass` [:L8] in BOTH directions in process. Across a boundary the outbound half would place a
#      database password into a response body, and therefore into any log that records responses and into
#      every characterization recording of one. The field is narrowed to WRITE-ONLY: accepted inbound,
#      never echoed in a response, never logged, never captured. Nothing resembling `logpass`, `logid`, a
#      database password or a broker credential appears in this file, and the generalisation is stated
#      once so a field not yet enumerated is still governed: any field whose value is a credential, a key,
#      or a statement containing interpolated literals is write-only inbound and redacted outbound, on
#      every boundary this refactor creates.
#
# 6.5  NO KEY MATERIAL FOR THE CRYPTOGRAPHIC SURFACE -- NO AES KEY, NO IV, NO RSA KEY, NO HMAC KEY, NO
#      PASSPHRASE. The one exception is stated at the end of this entry and is the signing key in
#      section 1; everything the C-02 crypto contract touches is reached by REFERENCE and never by value.
#      The legacy cryptographic surface takes its key as an ordinary in-parameter on every keyed
#      operation -- the keyed hash family at [ws_objects/pfw.crypto.pbl.src/n_crypto.sru:L23-L26], the
#      symmetric families at [:L30-L61], and, within the RSA family at [:L62-L73], the two operations that
#      take a PRIVATE key as an in-parameter: decrypt at [:L66-L69] and sign at [:L70-L71]. (Encrypt and
#      verify in that same range take the public key, which is not secret.) In one address space that
#      is unremarkable; on a wire the same signature would put key bytes into request bodies, into logs
#      and into recordings. The contract therefore NARROWS it: a caller passes an opaque key REFERENCE and
#      Security resolves it against its own configured key store, so there is no request field into which
#      a key could be placed. RSA key generation returns the public key and a reference to the private
#      key, never the private key itself. Consequently no key material is configured from this file --
#      only Security's own signing key, which is the single entry in section 1.
#
# 6.6  NO VARIABLE THAT NO TYPED OPTION CAN READ -- SPECIFICALLY, NO ABSOLUTE JWKS URL AND NO
#      PRE-ASSEMBLED SQLITE URI.
#      Both were once declared here and both were dead: `SECURITY_JWKS_URL` had no reader on any of the
#      three verifiers, and a `Sqlite:Uri` setting does not exist because Persistence composes the URI
#      from five discrete parts. A variable the manifest interpolates into a container that no option
#      binds is worse than a missing one -- it reads as configuration, an operator edits it expecting an
#      effect, and nothing changes. Where each replacement lives: the key-set address is derived per
#      service and the derivation is written out under SECURITY_BASE_URL in section 1; the storage
#      settings are enumerated under PERSISTENCE_SQLITE_DATA_DIRECTORY in section 2. The general rule this
#      is an instance of is the parity rule in the header -- every declared variable names the
#      configuration key it maps onto, and a name that resolves to nothing is the defect that rule exists
#      to catch.
#
# 6.7  NO `GATEWAY_BASE_URL` -- IT BELONGS TO THE END-TO-END SUITE, NOT TO ORCHESTRATION.
#      `tests/e2e/playwright.config.ts` reads `GATEWAY_BASE_URL` from the HOST environment and falls back
#      to `https://localhost:5105` -- https, matching the only listener Gateway binds. (An earlier
#      revision of this note wrote that fallback as `http`; the config has always used `https`, and the
#      attached environment's plaintext form is registered as **D7** in `docs/BUILD.md` section 1.6.) That is a test-runner setting, read on the host by Playwright rather
#      than interpolated into the Compose manifest, so declaring it here would be dead configuration that
#      misleads an operator into thinking it affects the stack. There is NO `tests/e2e/.env.example`
#      either -- an earlier revision of this template said the variable had its own template beneath
#      `tests/e2e/`; it does not, and no file was omitted (C-K). The fallback in
#      `playwright.config.ts` is the documented default and the suite needs nothing set to use it; the
#      variable is exported in the shell when a run must target a different host.
####################################################################################################


####################################################################################################
# 7. OPERATIONAL FOLLOW-UPS -- TWO ACTIONS NO AMOUNT OF CODE GENERATION CAN DISCHARGE
#
# A repository-wide sweep found ELEVEN sites, not the three that were expected: EIGHT IN-SOURCE
# HARDCODED-SECRET SITES plus THREE BINARY SCAN SITES, and the two categories are not the same thing.
# The eight in-source sites carry real material -- private keys, certificates, credentials -- and every
# one of them lies in the test, demo or legacy-browser-asset regions, all read-only, all mapping to
# deferred capability areas. So the remediation posture is NEVER REPLICATE, DOCUMENT, AND ROTATE, and
# never "edit the legacy file".
#
# THE THREE BINARY SITES ARE NOT ALL EMBEDDED MATERIAL, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES
# WHETHER THERE IS ANYTHING TO DO. Only `sciter.dll` actually embeds vendored trust material -- 12 bodies
# behind 13 markers, one marker being a body-less `CERTIFICATE` string inside a format string. `blink.dll`
# and `pfwx.dll` are MARKER-ONLY informational hits: a body-less `PUBLIC KEY` marker used for
# public-key-pinning output, and two body-less `PRIVATE KEY` markers inside diagnostic message text. Two
# of the three sites therefore contain no embedded material whatsoever, and calling all three "embedded"
# would overstate the exposure. All three are informational and none is source-remediable.
#
# No value from any of the eleven is reproduced here, in any .NET file, or in any document.
# docs/SECRETS.md carries every locator, severity and this same classification.
#
# Two of those findings need a human with access to a system outside this repository. They are recorded
# here as well as there so they travel with the secret roster instead of being lost in a document:
#
#   FOLLOW-UP 1 -- A LIVE BROKER CREDENTIAL TRIPLE. TREAT AS COMPROMISED AND ROTATE.
#   One legacy test object carries, inside a trailing comment block, a broker host address together with
#   an account name indicating administrative intent and that account's cleartext password. Rotation must
#   be performed by THE OWNER OF THAT BROKER, who is also the only party able to establish whether the
#   credential still authenticates. Until that confirmation exists, treat the credential as LIVE and the
#   repository as disclosing it -- the safe default, not a verified fact. Revoking the separate committed
#   identity that concerns the same messaging estate belongs to the same conversation.
#
#   FOLLOW-UP 2 -- A SHARED CONFIGURATION-ENCRYPTION KEY. ROTATE *WITH* A MIGRATION.
#   One legacy configuration object hardcodes a symmetric key and offers an override parameter -- but its
#   one- and two-argument entry points pass an empty value and therefore retain the literal, so EVERY
#   deployment that adopted the object unchanged shares one settings-encryption key. Because the object
#   provides NO re-encryption path, rotation alone would orphan every value already encrypted under the
#   old key. The action is rotation PLUS a migration that decrypts existing values with the old key and
#   re-encrypts them with the new one, per deployment, before the old key is withdrawn.
#
# THE CAVEAT THAT APPLIES TO BOTH, AND TO EVERY OTHER SITE IN THE REGISTER: material committed to version
# control must be considered exposed for as long as the history is retained. It is present in every clone
# and every fetch that has ever occurred. Rotation at the source system is the only effective remedy --
# it is the one action that makes disclosed material worthless rather than merely harder to find.
####################################################################################################
